C = [
("h1", "2. Romans 5 in Its Place"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 5:12–21 is the end of one argument and the opening of another, and the way it is read depends on what the reader thinks Paul has just finished saying. This chapter reads the argument from Romans 1:18 to 5:11, so that when the comparison of the two men begins, the reader knows what it is a comparison for."),
("sub", "2.1 The wrath and the charge (1:18–3:20)"),
("p", "Paul begins the body of the letter with a charge. “For the wrath of God is revealed from heaven against all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men, who hold the truth in unrighteousness” (Romans 1:18). He describes men who knew God and “glorified him not as God, neither were thankful” (Romans 1:21), and he says three times that God “gave them up” (Romans 1:24, 26, 28). He turns in chapter 2 to the man who judges another, and tells him, “Therefore thou art inexcusable, O man, whosoever thou art that judgest” (Romans 2:1). God “will render to every man according to his deeds” (Romans 2:6), and “there is no respect of persons with God” (Romans 2:11)."),
("p", "The charge is gathered up at the end of chapter 3: “we have before proved both Jews and Gentiles, that they are all under sin” (Romans 3:9). The verdict follows. “Therefore by the deeds of the law there shall no flesh be justified in his sight: for by the law is the knowledge of sin” (Romans 3:20). The passage Paul has been building is a charge against all men, and its conclusion is that “all the world may become guilty before God” (Romans 3:19)."),
("sub", "2.2 The righteousness of God (3:21–4:25)"),
("p", "Then comes the turn. “But now the righteousness of God without the law is manifested, being witnessed by the law and the prophets” (Romans 3:21). It is “by faith of Jesus Christ unto all and upon all them that believe: for there is no difference: For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God” (Romans 3:22–23). Those who are justified are justified “freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus” (Romans 3:24), and God is shown to be “just, and the justifier of him which believeth in Jesus” (Romans 3:26)."),
("p", "Chapter 4 argues the point from the record of Abraham. “Abraham believed God, and it was counted unto him for righteousness” (Romans 4:3). Paul sets the man who works beside the man who “worketh not, but believeth on him that justifieth the ungodly” (Romans 4:5), and he ends the chapter with the statement that Jesus “was delivered for our offences, and was raised again for our justification” (Romans 4:25). The two words that close the chapter, “offences” and “justification,” are the two words that open the comparison of the two men in chapter 5."),
("sub", "2.3 What the first eleven verses of chapter 5 say"),
("p", "Romans 5:1–11 draws the results. The chapter opens with “Therefore being justified by faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ” (Romans 5:1). It speaks of access, of rejoicing “in hope of the glory of God” (Romans 5:2), and of “tribulation worketh patience” (Romans 5:3). Then it comes to the cross. “For when we were yet without strength, in due time Christ died for the ungodly” (Romans 5:6). “But God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us” (Romans 5:8)."),
("p", "The verses carry a sequence that Paul states twice. “Much more then, being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath through him” (Romans 5:9). “For if, when we were enemies, we were reconciled to God by the death of his Son, much more, being reconciled, we shall be saved by his life” (Romans 5:10). The pattern is “if…much more.” What has been done by the death is the ground for what shall be done by the life. The same pattern governs the comparison that follows. In Romans 5:15 and 17 the “much more” returns."),
("tbl", "The words the first eleven verses give to the comparison.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 5:1", "“being justified by faith, we have peace with God”", "Justification and peace are the starting point"),
    ("Rom 5:8", "“while we were yet sinners, Christ died for us”", "The condition of those for whom he died"),
    ("Rom 5:9", "“being now justified by his blood, we shall be saved from wrath”", "The pattern “much more” begins"),
    ("Rom 5:10", "“much more, being reconciled, we shall be saved by his life”", "The death is the ground; the life carries it on"),
    ("Rom 5:11", "“we also joy in God through our Lord Jesus Christ, by whom we have now received the atonement”", "“Received” is the word that returns in 5:17"),
  ], [0.14, 0.52, 0.34]),
("sub", "2.4 The word “Wherefore”"),
("p", "Verse 12 begins with “Wherefore.” The word ties what follows to what has been said, and the reader is entitled to ask to which part. The nearest connection is to the verses just read. Paul has said that those who have been justified and reconciled will be saved, and he now wants to show why the one act of the cross can do so much. He does it by going back to the first man. The man through whom sin and death came into the world is set beside the man through whom the gift came, so that the greatness of the gift may be seen against the size of the harm."),
("p", "That is the reading this volume takes. It is not the only one, and some writers tie the “Wherefore” to the whole of the argument from 1:18. Both readings agree on the main point: the passage that follows is a comparison for the sake of what Christ’s act did, and it is placed at the end of an argument that has shown all men to be under sin and shown the righteousness of God to be given freely."),
("sub", "2.5 What the passage names and what it does not"),
("p", "One more observation belongs at the head of the passage. Romans 5:12–21 names the first man, names Moses once as a point in time, and names Jesus Christ. It does not name a nation, a covenant, an assembly or a program. The words are “all men,” “many,” “the world.” The volume therefore reads the passage as speaking about men as men: how sin and death reached them, and how the free gift reached them. It does not read the passage as being about one company of people rather than another, and it does not assign it to any one of the groups Paul addresses elsewhere in his letters."),
("p", "That reading is not a convenience. It follows from what the passage says, and from what the passage leaves out. A reader who wishes to find in Romans 5:12–21 a statement about Israel, or about the assembly, will have to supply the names. The volume declines to do so."),
]
